aking in the automotive industry.\",4\r\n1974,US,\"**Entity: United States (US)**\n\nThe United States (US) is a prominent country located in North America, recognized for its substantial influence on global economics and politics. It serves as a critical reference point in various economic analyses, particularly in the context of investment functions, such as the Grunfeld investment function. This function is instrumental in understanding the investment behavior of firms, especially in relation to their output and capital stock.\n\nThe Grunfeld investment function is a specific econometric model that captures the relationship between a firm's investment decisions and its economic environment. It is typically expressed in the form of a regression equation, which can be represented as:\n\n\\[ I_{it} = \\alpha + \\beta_1 K_{it-1} + \\beta_2 Y_{it} + \\epsilon_{it} \\]\n\nwhere \\( I_{it} \\) denotes the investment of firm \\( i \\) at time \\( t \\), \\( K_{it-1} \\) is the capital stock of the firm at the previous time period, \\( Y_{it} \\) represents the output of the firm, and \\( \\epsilon_{it} \\) is the error term. The coefficients \\( \\alpha \\), \\( \\beta_1 \\), and \\( \\beta_2 \\) are estimated through regression analysis.\n\nThe importance of the US in this context lies in its diverse and dynamic economy, which provides a rich dataset for empirical analysis. The Grunfeld investment function, when applied to US data, allows researchers to explore how firms adjust their investment in response to changes in output and capital stock, thereby providing insights into broader economic trends and firm behavior.\n\nIn econometric modeling, several assumptions underpin the validity of the Grunfeld investment function. These include linearity, independence of errors, homoscedasticity (constant variance of errors), and the absence of multicollinearity among the independent variables. Violating these assumptions can lead to biased estimates and incorrect inferences, 